<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Sunny!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Use basic words or phrases to identify time words. Interactive: Exchange simple questions and answers about the current day.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can count weather features up to 20, with help (e.g., "Let's count the clouds! One, two, three."). Can answer simple questions about the weather, using one- or two-word responses (e.g., responding "cold" when asked, "What is the weather today?"). Can express simple feelings or reactions to weather using words and gestures (e.g., saying "I'm hot." while fanning themselves or "I'm cold." with a shivering gesture).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of prior knowledge related to the theme "It's Sunny!" and scenario "Nice Weather Today!" using visuals and TPR.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Good Morning/Afternoon" song that includes simple weather gestures (e.g., waving hands for "windy", making a circle with arms for "sunny"). Students imitate gestures and sounds. Teacher holds up flashcards of previously learned weather words (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and asks, "What is this?" Students respond chorally or individually with one word. Teacher also revises numbers 1-5 by counting fingers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard for "sunny." Teacher says, "Sunny!" loudly and clearly, pointing to the word on the card. Then, teacher makes a big circle with arms and smiles, saying, "It is sunny today! I am happy!" Teacher also demonstrates "It is raining!" with fingers wiggling down and a sad face, and "I am cold!" by shivering. Teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining" and the initial /w/ in "weather" through repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a "rainy" flashcard and asks, "Is it sunny?" (Expected response: "No" or "Rainy!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to their shivers and asks, "Am I hot or cold?" (Expected response: "Cold!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!" and the Theme: "It's Sunny!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large weather flashcards (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, hot, cold), a simple weather chart on the board (drawing/symbol for each weather type), a short audio clip or song about daily weather (optional, very short and repetitive).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Talking about weather: Describe simple weather conditions (e.g., "It is sunny.") and describe simple reactions to weather (e.g., "I'm cold." or "I'm hot."). Asking questions about weather: Ask basic questions, such as "What is the weather today?" to learn about daily conditions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a large picture of a sunny day and asks, "Is it sunny or rainy?" Students point to the sun and say "Sunny!" (choral response).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to the weather chart and asks, "What is the weather today?" Teacher models, "It is sunny today." Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then acts out being hot and says, "I am hot." The teacher points to a "sunny" picture and asks, "What makes me hot?" (Expected response: "Sun!" or "Sunny!").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher explicitly introduces the target question "What is the weather today?" and the answer "It is [weather adjective]." and reaction "I am [feeling]." using a clear, slow voice and gestures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking. Students practice target grammar structures and vocabulary in controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present continuous (e.g., "It is raining."), Subject + verb + adjective (e.g., "It is sunny."), WH-questions (e.g., "What is the weather today?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, warm, cold, hot, day, numbers 1-20 (for counting features).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /s/ in "sunny," /r/ in "raining," initial /w/ in "weather."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Choral Repetition & TPR):</b> The teacher displays various weather flashcards. For each card, the teacher points and asks, "What is the weather today?" The class chorally responds, "It is [sunny/rainy/cloudy/windy]." Then, the teacher models a reaction with a gesture, "I am [hot/cold]," and students repeat chorally with the gesture. E.g., Teacher shows "sunny," asks "What is the weather today?" Class: "It is sunny!" Teacher: "I am hot!" Class: "I am hot!" Teacher specifically encourages clear pronunciation of /s/, /r/, and /w/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Picture prompts & Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher prepares small picture cards showing different weather conditions and simple feelings (e.g., a sun and a sweating face, clouds and a shivering person). The teacher holds up a card and prompts, "It is _______. I am _______." Students complete the sentence orally, using words like "sunny" and "hot" or "cloudy" and "cold." The teacher helps by providing the first word or gesturing. For counting, the teacher shows a picture with 5 suns and asks, "How many suns?" Students count aloud, "One, two, three, four, five suns!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Simple Q&A):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two different weather flashcards. Student A asks, "What is the weather today?" Student B looks at their card and responds, "It is [weather word]!" Student B then asks, "And you, what is the weather?" Student A responds based on their card. The teacher circulates, provides sentence frames on the board ("What is the weather today?", "It is ____."), and offers support and correction on pronunciation. Students are encouraged to use gestures when responding about their feelings (e.g., "I am hot!" with fanning gesture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!" and Theme: "It's Sunny!" Focus on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Weather Reporter" Role-Play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains that students will be "weather reporters" and report the weather to their classmates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of large weather picture cards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student from each group takes a turn being the "Weather Reporter." They pick a card, show it to their group, and say, "Good morning, everyone! Today, it is [weather]! I am [hot/cold/cool]." They must use a gesture for their feeling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The other students in the group act as the "audience." They can respond with simple phrases like "Oh, sunny!" or "Me too!" (if they feel the same), or just clap.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, encouraging students, providing prompts if needed, and gently correcting major errors that impede communication. The focus is on getting students to speak freely and interact using the target language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher also encourages groups to count features on a chosen weather card (e.g., "How many clouds do you see? One, two...").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check student learning and application of the target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will verbally answer a simple question from the teacher about the weather and express a feeling, either individually or in a pair with a peer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist for each student):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Can pronounce /s/, /r/, /w/ in target words clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses at least two weather words (e.g., "sunny," "rainy").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Can form a simple sentence "It is [weather]" and "I am [feeling]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates in pair/group interaction (e.g., asks or answers "What is the weather today?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show thumbs up for "Yes, I can talk about the weather!" or thumbs down for "I need more practice." Additionally, they draw a happy face if they liked talking about the weather or a sad face if not.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflection on the lesson's effectiveness and planning for future adjustments.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite weather word today?" (Students can point to a flashcard or say the word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like asking your friend, 'What is the weather today?'" (Yes/No response with a gesture).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, choral repetition, the "Weather Reporter" role-play).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to further support speaking, especially for shy students or those struggling with question formation? (e.g., more structured sentence frames, repetition of numbers 1-20 in weather contexts, smaller group activities).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on the vocabulary and simple sentences used today. Students will practice writing "It is [weather]" and "I am [feeling]" sentences based on pictures, incorporating initial letter sounds and familiar vocabulary words.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>